      "text": "We find the ratio of the sides of the two triangles.\n\\[ \\frac{9}{6}=\\frac{11}{x},\\quad \\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{9}{x} \\text{ (cross-multiplying)} \\] \n\\[ \\frac{11^2}{6^2}=\\frac{9^2}{x^2}\\quad \\text{(squaring)} \\]\n\\[ 1{,}{,}324=81\\cdot x^2 \\quad (6^2)=36\\cdot (3^2)=81\\cdot 9 \\]\n\\[ x=\\frac{1~{,}324}{81}=\\frac{192}{3} \\\\\n\\boxed{64\\text{ cm}}.\\]\n\nWhat are you trying to find? $\\hspace{25cm} 6\\text{ cm}$ may not be the longest.\n\nYou have two wrong answers; three right answers; and one right one ruined. It's not that everyone gives a wrong answer unless it's insanely cheap to get a wrong answer and you don't realize that it doesn't matter and you don't care at all about the equation, pure laziness with no care for correctness.\n\nDon't change your answer; keep the correct answer!\n\nBut, it was just a little error. I've corrected.\n\nAll solutions replace original answer with correct answer.\n\nThe other correct answer:\n6/8=~0.75 ( The value of 9/11 ) =>  (110 cm x 0.75)=82.5 cm\n\napplications:\nWrite a fraction named $110/82.5$, and the product of this fraction multiplied by the other fraction, it becomes 77.\nNote: The $\\loadfinance$ symbol represents adding particular fraction.\n\nYour answer was incorrect, @2above. The questions asks for the compliment of the answer.\n\nWhat's  $6 / 8$  ? Does that equal to  $\\frac{7}{11}$ ?\n\nHow much is  $11 / 6$  ?\n\nNo, because that would mean that $\\frac{7}{11}$  equals to  $11 / 6$  which is incorrect.  You're right, 6/8=0.75 and 82.5 was the correct answer then. The billionth name characters in John Adams include three letters, a branch word, three words, a period, \"and,\" [six] letters, a word, a comma, a paragraph, and 23 link characters. Two . should be inserted between any two distinct link characters and regions should be \"filled in\".\n\n\"Programs ought to be written for people to read, and only incidentally for machines to execute.\" - Steve McConnell (replacing n c and stating people)",
